      "type": "paragraph",
      "sourceText": "introduce substantial cupping or banding artifacts in reconstructed images [17, 18]. The expanded detector coverage in the dual wide-coverage CT system increases both the probability and magnitude of such events. In phantom measurements performed under identical scan protocols, the total scattered radiation intensity measured with the 16-cm collimation width was approx. 1.2–1.8 times that measured with the 4-cm collimation width (Supplementary Information S1) [19]. To address this, the dual wide-coverage system implements a dedicated correction method that combines physical modeling with empirical calibration, executed through two sequential steps: scatter shape estimation and scatter intensity estimation. Step 1—shape estimation. A simplified single-scatter model is used to derive the relative spatial distribution of cross-scatter. Based on a preliminary segmented image, ray tracing is performed to compute attenuation path lengths and Compton scattering probability at each candidate scatter point. Contributions from all points are summed and normalized to obtain a scatter shape estimate across the detector array. Step 2—intensity estimation. The absolute scatter intensity is determined using an empirical model derived from phantom measurements across varying object sizes and geometries. For each projection view, the peak scatter intensity is obtained from this model and used to scale the normalized scatter shape to absolute signal strength. The estimated cross-scatter signal CS is then subtracted from the measured data: \u0012 \u0013 \u0012 \u0013",
