      "sourceText": "were visually assessed for the presence of residual crossscatter artifacts. For the spatial resolution assessment, a phantom containing a 0.1-mm diameter tungsten wire was scanned using the dual wide-coverage mode with the following parameters: 16 cm collimation, 1.0 s rotation time, and a volume CT dose index of 47.6 mGy. Images were reconstructed with a field of view of 50 mm, a slice thickness of 0.5 mm, and a slice increment of 0.5 mm using a sharp kernel. The modulation transfer function (MTF) was derived from the line spread function obtained from the wire phantom. MTF analysis was conducted at two representative detector positions: the central slice (8 cm from the detector edge) and a peripheral slice (2 cm from the detector edge), both with and without cross-scatter correction. For the low-contrast detectability assessment, the CTP515 module of the Catphan 700 phantom was scanned using the same acquisition protocol as described above. This module contains multiple sets of cylindrical low-contrast inserts ranging in diameter from 2 to 15 mm, with nominal contrast levels of 0.3%, 0.5%, and 1.0%, designed to assess the ability to distinguish objects with subtle differences in attenuation. Images were reconstructed with a field of view of 200 mm, a slice thickness of 1.0 mm, and a slice increment of 1.0 mm using a soft kernel. The minimum detectable insert diameter was determined for both the central slice and the peripheral slice, with and without cross-scatter correction.\n\nTest of temporal resolution",
