    {
      "id": "page-2-region-4",
      "pageIndex": 2,
      "order": 6,
      "type": "paragraph",
      "sourceText": "Temporal resolution was tested using a custom-built dynamic phantom capable of periodic oscillation. The phantom features a pair of 15‑cm oscillating arms that swing symmetrically, with a fixed amplitude of 40°. The angular velocity is proportionally adjusted according to the simulated heart rate (e.g., 0.45 r/s at 100 bpm). An optical switch at the horizontal position triggers a programmable simulated ECG signal for the CT scanner upon each return of the arm, synchronizing motion phase with the ECG. The arm includes a 61‑ms rest phase at the horizontal position to simulate the diastolic rest period. In this study, two motion patterns were programmed: a regular rhythm mode with constant angular velocity (100 bpm) corresponding to sinus rhythm, and an arrhythmia simulation mode with alternating angular velocities (100 bpm and 75 bpm alternately) to mimic beat‑to‑beat heart rate variability in arrhythmia. Through analysis of CT images of the oscillating arm, the degree of motion blurring can be evaluated, allowing assessment of the system’s temporal resolution across different simulated cardiac motion patterns. Detailed specifications of the phantom are provided in the Supplementary",
